  "messages": [
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm working with somatic mutation profiles from 64 colorectal tumours sequenced on a targeted 400-gene panel, and I also have gene lengths, a cancer gene list and a pathway GMT file. Could you cluster the tumours into mutation-driven subgroups based on which pathways are hit? Please try between 3 and 6 clusters."
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "What I understood you are asking for:\n1. \"Could you cluster the tumours into mutation-driven subgroups based on which pathways are hit?\" -- the request does not say whether one item may belong to several groups at once: if one group each: SAMBAR; if several groups at once: no registered workflow gives this.\n2. \"Please try between 3 and 6 clusters.\" -- the request does not say whether one item may belong to several groups at once: if one group each: SAMBAR; if several groups at once: no registered workflow gives this.\n\nBefore I choose a workflow, please tell me: Should each gene, regulator or sample belong to one group only (one group each), or may one belong to several groups at once?\n\nNo workflow has been selected and nothing runs before you answer.\n\nNo files were inspected and no analysis ran."
    },
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Previous NetZoo goal: I'm working with somatic mutation profiles from 64 colorectal tumours sequenced on a targeted 400-gene panel, and I also have gene lengths, a cancer gene list and a pathway GMT file. Could you cluster the tumours into mutation-driven subgroups based on which pathways are hit? Please try between 3 and 6 clusters.\nUser follow-up: One subgroup per tumour is fine; I only need to know which cluster each one lands in."
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "What I understood you are asking for:\n1. \"Could you cluster the tumours into mutation-driven subgroups based on which pathways are hit?\" -- available from SAMBAR.\n2. \"Please try between 3 and 6 clusters.\" -- available from SAMBAR.\n3. \"One subgroup per tumour is fine; I only need to know which cluster each one lands in.\" -- available from SAMBAR.\n\nSelected path: **SAMBAR**.\n\nYour question asks for a cohort-level sample cluster assignment. **SAMBAR** fits that result and scale.\n\nWhy this recommendation:\n\nGene length normalization addresses the greater opportunity for mutations in longer genes; gene/exon sizes are therefore required, not just the mutation matrix.\n\nPathway aggregation turns gene mutation scores into pathway-by-sample scores; sample distances are then calculated from those profiles, and clustering assigns sample labels. These are separate artifacts, not patient-specific mutation networks.\n\nWhat you asked about:\n\n- \"I only need to know which cluster each one lands in.\" — Subtype labels can be compared with clinical variables -- survival between subtypes, for example; that needs a clinical table keyed by the same sample IDs. The pathway mutation scores show which pathways separate the subtypes.\n\n**SAMBAR** — Subtype somatic-mutation samples through pathway mutation scores with SAMBAR. SAMBAR reduces sparse mutation data to pathway-level scores with gene-length and, when configured, patient mutation-burden normalization. Patient distances and subtype labels depend on the downstream clustering settings; they are distinct from gene communities in a regulatory network. This is pathway aggregation followed by distance-based clustering, not non-negative matrix factorization; a mutation score does not establish permanent functional loss or equivalence of every mutation in a pathway.\n\nRequired workflow inputs:\n\n- `mutation_file`: somatic mutation matrix\n- `exon_size_file`: gene/exon-size CSV\n- `cancer_gene_file`: cancer-gene list\n- `pathway_file`: GMT pathway file\n\nOutputs:\n\n- `gene_mutation_scores`: Sample-by-gene mutation scores, not pathway scores.\n- `pathway_mutation_matrix`: Pathway-by-sample mutation scores, not cluster labels.\n- `sample_cluster_assignment`: Sample-to-cluster labels, separate from score and distance matrices.\n- `sample_distance_matrix`: Pairwise sample distances, not cluster labels.\n\nAsk for the workflow's controls and defaults if you want to set them.\n\nThis is workflow guidance only; no execution was authorized. No files were inspected and no analysis ran."
    }
  ],
